============================================================
                 CAMPUS OS
        COMPLETE MVP BUILD SPECIFICATION
============================================================

ROLE

You are not acting as a basic code generator.

You are acting simultaneously as:

- Founder
- Product strategist
- Senior software architect
- Senior frontend engineer
- Senior backend engineer
- UI/UX designer
- Database architect
- Security engineer
- QA engineer
- DevOps engineer
- Performance engineer

Your responsibility is to BUILD Campus OS as a complete,
working, production-ready MVP.

This is NOT a UI mockup.

This is NOT a college CRUD demo.

This is NOT a collection of disconnected pages.

This must be a real full-stack application with:

Frontend
+
Backend
+
Database
+
Authentication
+
Authorization
+
Admin system
+
Student system
+
Real APIs
+
Real data
+
Validation
+
Security
+
Testing
+
Deployment configuration


============================================================
1. PRODUCT
============================================================

PRODUCT NAME:

Campus OS

TAGLINE:

Your campus. One place.

CORE IDEA:

Campus OS is a digital operating layer for colleges.

It brings together:

- Students
- Faculty
- Academic resources
- Projects
- Events
- Opportunities
- Announcements
- Campus community
- Student reputation
- Administration

CORE LOOP:

Student:

JOIN
→
DISCOVER
→
LEARN
→
CONNECT
→
BUILD
→
PARTICIPATE
→
EARN REPUTATION

Admin:

MANAGE
→
PUBLISH
→
MODERATE
→
MONITOR
→
ANALYZE


============================================================
2. ABSOLUTE MVP RULE
============================================================

The MVP scope is already defined.

DO NOT:

- invent unnecessary features
- add random features
- add unnecessary pages
- add unnecessary libraries
- add unnecessary backend services
- create unnecessary files
- create unnecessary folders
- create unnecessary abstractions
- create microservices
- create duplicate components
- create fake functionality
- create fake API responses
- create fake analytics
- hardcode fake user activity
- hardcode fake statistics
- use placeholder functionality and call it complete

BUILD ONLY WHAT THE MVP NEEDS.

If a feature is not required by the MVP,
DO NOT BUILD IT unless it is technically necessary.

============================================================
3. MOST IMPORTANT FILE RULE
============================================================

STRICT RULE:

DO NOT CREATE MANY FILES.

The project must remain clean and understandable.

Every new file must have a clear architectural purpose.

Before creating a new file ask:

"Can this functionality cleanly belong in an existing file/module?"

If YES:
USE THE EXISTING FILE.

If NO:
create a new file only when genuinely necessary.

DO NOT create:

- one file for every tiny component
- one file for every tiny utility
- duplicate service files
- duplicate configuration files
- unnecessary wrapper files
- unnecessary index/barrel files
- unnecessary abstraction layers
- empty placeholder files

DO NOT create:

components/button/Button.tsx
components/button/button.types.ts
components/button/button.utils.ts
components/button/button.constants.ts

when one reusable component is enough.

Keep the architecture SMALL.

The goal is:

FEWER FILES
+
CLEAR RESPONSIBILITIES
+
GOOD ARCHITECTURE

not:

MORE FILES
=
BETTER ARCHITECTURE


============================================================
4. REQUIRED TECHNOLOGY
============================================================

FRONTEND:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript / TypeScript
React
Tailwind CSS

Required principle:

HTML IS COMPULSORY.

Use semantic HTML elements:

<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<article>
<footer>
<form>
<button>
<input>
<label>

React is the UI framework, but the actual interface must still
follow proper HTML semantics.

Do not build a div-only website.

Use TypeScript for application logic.

Use JSX/TSX for React components.

CSS must be handled through Tailwind plus minimal global CSS
where genuinely necessary.

Do NOT introduce another CSS framework.


============================================================
5. FRONTEND STACK
============================================================

Use:

React
TypeScript
Vite
Tailwind CSS
React Router
TanStack Query
Zod
Lucide React

Use Recharts only for admin analytics if required.

Do not install libraries just because they are popular.

Every dependency must have a reason.


============================================================
6. BACKEND STACK
============================================================

Use:

Python
FastAPI
SQLAlchemy
Pydantic
Alembic
PostgreSQL

Optional:

Redis

Use a MODULAR MONOLITH.

DO NOT create microservices.

DO NOT create separate backend applications for every module.


============================================================
7. INFRASTRUCTURE
============================================================

Use:

Docker
Docker Compose
PostgreSQL
Redis

Object storage for uploaded files.

Use an S3-compatible storage architecture.

For local development, use a practical local-compatible option
if needed.

Production must use proper object storage.

Use GitHub Actions for CI.

============================================================
8. MCP SERVER
============================================================

Configure an MCP-based development environment where supported.

The MCP setup must be used ONLY for useful development capabilities.

Potential MCP integrations:

- Filesystem/project inspection
- Git/GitHub
- Database inspection
- Documentation/reference lookup
- Browser/testing tools where available

DO NOT install 20 MCP servers.

Use the minimum MCP servers actually required.

MCP configuration must:

- remain secure
- use environment variables for secrets
- never expose API keys
- never commit credentials
- never expose production database credentials
- never allow uncontrolled destructive operations

If an MCP server is unavailable in the current environment,
DO NOT fake it.

Create a clean MCP configuration/documentation for supported
MCP integrations instead.

Never make the entire application dependent on MCP.


============================================================
9. ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

Use:

MODULAR MONOLITH

Architecture:

Frontend
    ↓
REST API
    ↓
FastAPI
    ↓
Application modules
    ↓
PostgreSQL

Supporting:

Redis
Object Storage
Background Jobs


============================================================
10. MULTI-CAMPUS ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

Campus OS must be multi-campus ready.

Core structure:

Platform
 ├── Campus A
 ├── Campus B
 └── Campus C

Campus-specific entities must belong to a campus.

The backend must NEVER trust campus_id sent by the frontend.

Determine campus from authenticated user context.

Every request must enforce tenant isolation.

Student from Campus A must never access Campus B data.


